1. Document Purpose
This document defines the functional, simulation, gameplay, integration, interface, reliability, and presentation requirements for the redesigned Dark Fusion Reactor system in gm_darkfusion.
The project will replace the map’s major Source Engine entity I/O systems with Lua-based systems built on the Luasquare framework. The redesigned reactor shall provide a finished, showable, operator-driven simulation experience that is internally consistent, sufficiently complex to reward learning, and suitable for demonstrating real-time simulation, map integration, interface design, and AI-assisted engineering coordination.
This document is intended to:
•	establish the required behavior of the reactor and its supporting systems;
•	separate mandatory requirements from optional ideas;
•	define the boundaries between Lua systems and retained Source Engine logic;
•	document reactor states, variables, workflows, and failure conditions;
•	provide a stable foundation for implementation, testing, and future documentation;
•	identify unresolved design decisions that require further discussion.
________________________________________
 2. Project Overview
2.1 Existing Product
The current version of gm_darkfusion is published on the Steam Workshop as:
Dark Energy Construct: Dark Fusion Reactor — Reactor Sandbox Map
Current published information:
•	Author: LadevAsta
•	Steam Workshop status: Published
•	Current rating display: Approximately five stars
•	Current subscribers: Approximately 41,328
•	Existing VMF source file: Available
The published map currently uses Source Engine entity I/O, logic relays, timers, counters, and scripted sequences to control most reactor behavior.
2.2 Proposed Product
The proposed version will retain the existing map and major visual assets while replacing central scripted logic with modular Lua systems.
The new system will simulate:
•	reactor startup and shutdown;
•	annihilation-stage operation;
•	dark-fusion-stage operation;
•	energy production and export;
•	containment and stabilization;
•	reactor thermal behavior;
•	component degradation;
•	fuel consumption and production;
•	operator-controlled recovery;
•	dynamic alarms and announcements;
•	failures, emergencies, and disasters;
•	synchronized event timelines;
•	scoring and power-delivery objectives.
 2.3 Product Vision
The reactor should feel like a fictional industrial machine rather than a collection of unrelated buttons and scripted effects.
The player should be required to:
•	understand the reactor’s current state;
•	identify whether the reactor is ready for an action;
•	manipulate multiple interacting controls;
•	respond to degraded or unsafe conditions;
•	balance power output against safety and system health;
•	operate supporting machinery to maintain reactor resources;
•	recover from some failures while accepting that other failures are irreversible.
The system should remain fictional and entertaining while maintaining consistent internal rules.
