3. Definitions and Terminology
3.1 Core Terms
AN-Fuel
Collective term for the matter and antimatter consumed during the annihilation stage. Where necessary, requirements shall refer separately to matter fuel and antimatter fuel.
Annihilation Stage
The reactor stage in which matter-antimatter reactions generate power and prepare the core for dark-fusion ignition.
Dark Fusion Stage
The reactor’s primary high-output operating stage.
Energy Saturation
A measure of accumulated reactor energy relative to the core’s current ability to expel, convert, or contain that energy.
Influx
Energy or reaction input entering the dark-fusion process.
Outflux
Energy leaving the active reaction through export, expulsion, stabilization, waste heat, or other modeled processes.
Catalyzer
One of six reactor subsystems capable of operating in several modes, including supporting annihilation reactions and catalyzing dark fusion.
Stabilizer
The system responsible for maintaining the containment field, suppressing orbital influence, and controlling reactor stability.
Director Beam
A photonic beam used during reactor startup and annihilation-stage operation.
DECAOS
The facility’s automated announcer, operator-assistance, authorization, subtitle, warning, and event-coordination system.
Reactive Timeline
A timed sequence that can synchronize reactor actions, music, announcements, effects, and state changes while responding to predefined failure conditions.
Trip
An automatic protective shutdown or interruption of a subsystem after an unsafe condition is detected.
Superstructure Integrity
The overall structural and operational health of the reactor, stabilizer, supporting machinery, and surrounding installation.
Ending State
A terminal gameplay condition that concludes the current reactor session or scenario.
3.2 Requirement Keywords
The words shall, should, and may have the following meanings:
•	Shall: mandatory requirement;
•	Should: preferred behavior that may be changed with justification;
•	May: optional behavior or implementation choice.
