 4. Project Goals
4.1 Primary Goals
The project shall:
1.	replace major Source Engine reactor logic with Luasquare-controlled Lua modules;
2.	create a complete reactor lifecycle from offline state through startup, operation, shutdown, failure, and disaster;
3.	provide meaningful operator interaction during all major operating stages;
4.	make reactor behavior understandable through instruments, alarms, subtitles, visual feedback, and DECAOS announcements;
5.	allow reactor systems to interact through defined variables rather than isolated scripted events;
6.	support both guided automatic startup and fully interactive manual startup;
7.	preserve the visual identity and major environmental features of the existing map;
8.	provide enough depth to support repeated play and operator mastery;
9.	minimize unnecessary logic entities in the final compiled map;
10.	create a system architecture that can be documented, extended, tested, and maintained.
4.2 Secondary Goals
The project should:
•	demonstrate the capabilities of the Luasquare framework;
•	serve as a portfolio-quality software and game-systems project;
•	support cinematic events without making normal operation entirely scripted;
•	provide clear separation between simulation logic, map integration, audiovisual presentation, and operator interfaces;
•	retain compatibility with important legacy map elements where practical.
 4.3 Non-Goals
The initial release is not required to:
•	accurately simulate real-world nuclear physics;
•	reproduce an existing real reactor design;
•	support competitive multiplayer balancing;
•	simulate every pipe, electrical circuit, or mechanical component individually;
•	guarantee that every catastrophic event can be prevented after its irreversible threshold;
•	replace minor environmental systems that already function correctly through Source I/O;
•	provide a physically realistic planetary or orbital simulation.
