14. Superstructure and Damage Requirements
14.1 Integrity
Superstructure integrity shall be represented as a percentage from 0% to 100%, unless the implementation requires internal values above 100%.
14.2 Damage Sources
Integrity shall decrease when one or more of the following occur:
•	energy saturation exceeds safe limits;
•	temperature exceeds safe limits;
•	director-beam imprecision causes damage;
•	a containment overload occurs;
•	an emergency shutdown causes an explosion;
•	a Catalyzer failure applies structural damage;
•	a scripted or random damage event occurs;
•	another documented subsystem failure applies damage.
14.3 Integrity Effects
As integrity decreases, the system shall progressively alter reactor and map behavior.
Effects may include:
•	reduced subsystem effectiveness;
•	debris spawning;
•	increased camera or map shaking;
•	distant explosion sounds;
•	sparks and electrical failures;
•	alarm escalation;
•	smoke or fog changes;
•	display failures;
•	control delays;
•	incorrect sensor readings;
•	lower maximum safe power;
•	increased malfunction probability.
14.4 Integrity Recovery
DMG-001
Integrity shall recover only under defined conditions.
The default design assumption is that meaningful integrity recovery occurs only while the reactor is shut down.
The final specification shall define:
•	recovery delay;
•	recovery rate;
•	maximum recoverable integrity;
•	whether severe damage permanently reduces maximum integrity;
•	whether player maintenance can accelerate recovery;
•	whether ending-level damage is recoverable.
14.5 Integrity Thresholds
The system shall define named integrity thresholds such as:
•	stable;
•	damaged;
•	heavily damaged;
•	critical;
•	irreversible failure;
•	zero integrity.
Crossing each threshold shall generate an event and presentation response.
