15. Thermal Plant Requirements
15.1 Purpose
The secondary thermal plant shall convert a portion of reactor waste heat into useful power or transfer waste heat into the primordial sea.
The initial implementation may be simulation-only and does not require physical moving machinery.
15.2 Thermal Paths
The system shall support at least two heat-removal paths.
Power-Recovery Path
Reactor Waste Heat → Primordial Coolant Loop → Steam Generator Heat Exchanger → Hyperdense Steam Loop → Turbines → Condenser
Direct-Rejection Path
Reactor Waste Heat → Primordial Coolant Loop → Primordial Sea
15.3 Thermal Requirements
THERM-001
Reactor operation shall generate waste heat.
THERM-002
Waste heat should normally range from approximately 9,000 MWth to 80 GWth, subject to balancing.
THERM-003
Coolant circulation rate shall influence heat removal.
THERM-004
Insufficient heat removal shall increase reactor or subsystem temperature.
THERM-005
High temperature shall reduce subsystem effectiveness.
THERM-006
The power-recovery path shall convert some waste heat into electrical output.
THERM-007
The direct-rejection path shall remove heat without producing equivalent electrical output.
THERM-008
Direct rejection shall consume or lose primordial coolant according to a defined rate.
THERM-009
Primordial coolant production shall consume facility power.
THERM-010
Long-term direct rejection should increase primordial-sea temperature if that mechanic remains in scope.
15.4 Open Thermal Decisions
The following require definition:
•	whether coolant quantity is finite;
•	whether coolant can leak;
•	whether sea temperature affects the map;
•	turbine efficiency;
•	condenser capacity;
•	maximum coolant-loop flow;
•	consequences of steam-generator failure;
•	whether thermal power contributes to the 20 TW reactor rating.
________________________________________
16. Fuel and Enrichment Requirements
16.1 Fuel Types
The system shall model:
•	matter fuel;
•	antimatter fuel;
•	Catalyzer fuel cells;
•	stasis cells;
•	primordial coolant, if treated as a consumable resource.
16.2 Fuel Receptacles
The reactor shall contain three primary fuel-cell receptacles for startup preparation.
Each receptacle shall report:
•	occupied or empty;
•	inserted fuel-cell entity;
•	fuel-cell percentage;
•	valid or invalid fuel type;
•	locked or removable state.
16.3 Fuel Enrichment Towers
The map shall contain four Dark Fusion Fuel Enrichment Towers.
The towers shall have their own operating mechanics.
At minimum, the tower system shall define:
•	required inputs;
•	power consumption;
•	production time;
•	produced resource;
•	production capacity;
•	operating controls;
•	failure conditions;
•	visual state;
•	output collection method.
16.4 Fuel Requirements
FUEL-001
Startup readiness shall require sufficient AN-Fuel.
FUEL-002
Startup readiness shall require valid fuel cells in the required receptacles.
FUEL-003
Fuel shall not be consumed when its consuming subsystem is offline unless explicitly required.
FUEL-004
Fuel production shall consume time and facility resources.
FUEL-005
The operator shall receive advance warning before a required fuel resource reaches a forced-shutdown threshold.
FUEL-006
Fuel values displayed to clients shall remain synchronized with the authoritative server state.
