18. Normal Operation Requirements
18.1 Core Operator Objective
During normal operation, the operator shall balance reactor output, energy saturation, containment demand, thermal load, fuel availability, subsystem health, and external power quotas.
18.2 Influx and Outflux
OPER-001
The reactor shall calculate influx using a documented function.
OPER-002
The reactor shall calculate outflux using a documented function.
OPER-003
Influx and outflux shall be influenced by multiple reactor variables.
OPER-004
Increasing influx shall generally increase production and energy saturation.
OPER-005
Increasing stabilizer power shall generally reduce or control energy saturation while increasing reactor consumption.
OPER-006
The final effect of a control shall be communicated through instrumentation rather than hidden entirely from the operator.
18.3 Power Rating
The reactor is intended to be described as a 20 TW reactor.
The specification shall clarify whether 20 TW means:
•	nominal gross reaction output;
•	maximum safe export;
•	rated continuous export;
•	peak short-duration output;
•	or a fictional nameplate rating.
18.4 Power Export
finalEnergyExportTW shall represent the credited power delivered after internal consumption and losses.
A proposed relation is:
Final Export = Gross Reactor Output + Recovered Thermal Output - Stabilizer Consumption - Facility Consumption - Enrichment Consumption - Other Internal Loads
The final formula shall be defined during simulation design.
18.5 Power Quotas
QUOTA-001
Power quotas shall begin after the operator reports the reactor online through extension 7472, or after an equivalent automatic confirmation.
QUOTA-002
Quotas shall arrive at defined intervals.
QUOTA-003
Each quota shall define a requested power amount and duration.
QUOTA-004
Maintaining export at or above the quota shall increase score.
QUOTA-005
Operating above safe limits shall reduce credited score.
QUOTA-006
Unsafe overproduction shall not be rewarded more than safe quota fulfillment.
QUOTA-007
The operator shall be shown current quota, credited output, time remaining, and penalties.
________________________________________
19. Shutdown Requirements
19.1 Dark-Fusion Stall Shutdown
To leave the dark-fusion stage through controlled stalling:
1.	The operator shall reduce energy saturation below the defined stall threshold.
2.	DECAOS shall warn that the reactor is stalling.
3.	Dark fusion shall end after the stall condition remains valid for the required duration.
4.	The reactor shall transition to the annihilation stage.
5.	Automatic AN-Fuel injection may temporarily increase to sustain the annihilation stage.
SHUT-DF-001
The stall threshold and delay shall be documented.
SHUT-DF-002
The operator shall receive enough warning to distinguish an intended stall from an accidental one.
SHUT-DF-003
The transition shall not produce an impossible fuel or energy state.
19.2 Automatic Annihilation-Stage Shutdown
Automatic shutdown shall be available when:
•	the reactor is in the annihilation stage;
•	DECAOS is available;
•	the triple-lever system is operational;
•	no blocking emergency state exists.
The automatic shutdown should:
1.	disable automatic AN-Fuel injection;
2.	resolve unsafe antimatter dominance;
3.	increase matter injection until matter dominance is achieved;
4.	vent remaining hydrogen or matter fuel as required;
5.	shut down the director beam after net power becomes negative or reaches the defined threshold;
6.	weaken the containment field;
7.	turn off the stabilizer;
8.	retract reactor machinery;
9.	enter the offline state.
19.3 Manual Shutdown
A manual shutdown procedure shall be documented separately.
The manual procedure should require the operator to perform the automatic shutdown actions through individual controls.
19.4 Shutdown Safety
SHUT-001
The system shall prevent containment weakening while antimatter dominance remains above the safe threshold, unless an emergency procedure is active.
SHUT-002
The system shall warn the operator before a shutdown action creates a known explosion risk.
SHUT-003
Shutdown completion shall restore the correct map lighting, sounds, displays, and machinery positions.
________________________________________
20. Emergency Procedures
20.1 Antimatter Termination
Antimatter Termination is an emergency shutdown procedure for the annihilation stage.
The procedure shall:
•	cut off antimatter injection;
•	maximize matter injection;
•	force matter dominance;
•	stop or rapidly reduce the annihilation reaction;
•	cause a hydrogen explosion or equivalent pressure event;
•	overload the containment field;
•	damage superstructure integrity;
•	remain recoverable if the resulting damage does not cross an irreversible threshold.
EMERG-AT-001
Antimatter Termination shall not be available during the dark-fusion stage.
EMERG-AT-002
The interface shall clearly communicate that the procedure causes damage.
EMERG-AT-003
The procedure shall require deliberate activation to prevent accidental use.
20.2 Reactor Purge Sequence
The Reactor Purge Sequence shall occur automatically during Meltdown Stage 2 unless design changes make it manually initiated.
The sequence shall require operators to:
•	retrieve Stasis Cells;
•	install one Stasis Cell into each of the six Catalyzers;
•	complete installation before the scheduled stasis-laser firing.
Successful purge shall:
•	stop all active reactions;
•	severely damage or disable the citadel spinal superstructure;
•	produce a terminal ending due to major asset damage.
Failure shall transition the reactor to Meltdown Stage 3.
EMERG-PURGE-001
The purge sequence shall provide a visible countdown.
EMERG-PURGE-002
Each installed Stasis Cell shall be individually confirmed.
EMERG-PURGE-003
The purge sequence shall remain completable in multiplayer and single-player contexts.
EMERG-PURGE-004
The system shall define behavior when a Stasis Cell is removed after installation.
________________________________________
21. Failure and Disaster Requirements
21.1 Failure Classification
Failures shall be classified as:
•	warning;
•	recoverable fault;
•	severe recoverable fault;
•	trip condition;
•	emergency;
•	irreversible failure;
•	terminal disaster.
21.2 Runaway Fusion
Runaway Fusion shall begin when superstructure integrity reaches 0% during the dark-fusion stage.
It shall contain three stages.
Each stage shall define:
•	entry condition;
•	duration;
•	music;
•	DECAOS lines;
•	subtitles;
•	map effects;
•	operator objectives;
•	available controls;
•	transition condition;
•	recoverability;
•	ending behavior.
21.3 Meltdown Stage 1
Stage 1 should represent the initial runaway condition.
Possible operator objectives:
•	reduce influx;
•	maximize stabilization;
•	preserve Catalyzer functionality;
•	restore coolant circulation;
•	prevent progression to Stage 2.
The final design shall define whether Stage 1 is recoverable.
21.4 Meltdown Stage 2
Stage 2 shall initiate or prepare the Reactor Purge Sequence.
Stage 2 shall communicate:
•	remaining time;
•	Stasis Cell locations;
•	installed-cell count;
•	unavailable Catalyzers;
•	consequences of success and failure.
21.5 Meltdown Stage 3
Stage 3 shall represent an unrecoverable runaway-fusion ending.
The final sequence shall define:
•	reactor visual destruction;
•	map-wide effects;
•	DECAOS status;
•	player survival or death;
•	NPC behavior;
•	final score;
•	reset behavior.
21.6 Singularity Containment Breach
A singularity breach may be caused by:
•	event-horizon expansion during prolonged low-power dark-fusion operation;
•	singularity formation during ultra-high-power annihilation operation;
•	containment failure while a singularity exists.
The sequence shall:
1.	form or expose the singularity;
2.	consume or destroy the containment field;
3.	damage or destroy the Stabilizer arm;
4.	reduce orbital-influence suppression toward zero;
5.	trigger external commotion communicated through DECAOS;
6.	disable or destroy the DECAOS mainframe;
7.	cause the singularity to descend into the planetary core;
8.	enter a terminal ending state.
There shall be no recovery after the irreversible singularity-breach threshold.
The operator shall receive warnings before the irreversible threshold unless the sequence is caused by an intentionally immediate catastrophic action.
